<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dali Kingdom</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dali Kingdom was a state in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> in southwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, which lasted from the tenth to the thirteenth century. It was conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> in the 1250s. The conquest outflanked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> and brought Yunnan into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dali-kingdom.jpg" alt="Dali Kingdom" width="320" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dali_Kingdom.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Nanzhao was overthrown in 902, and three short-lived dynasties ruled its successor kingdoms in quick succession: Great Changhe, Great Tianxing and Great Yining. In 934 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duan-siping">Duan Siping</a>, an official in the Tonghai Area Command, organised a coalition of Nanzhao sympathisers and overthrew the Yang regime, and in 937 he seized power and established himself at Dali. The Duan clan professed Han ancestry, and Yuan records say they came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuwei">Wuwei</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>, which neither <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> nor Dali sources confirm. The kingdom took its name from Dali, which means marble in Chinese.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> were cordial, though Dali was essentially independent. The founder of the Song, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-song">Taizu</a>, declared the Dadu River the border and refused further claims, so as to avoid the costly wars the Tang had fought against Nanzhao. Dali established tribute relations in 982, but Song officials allowed it to offer tribute at court only three times. Its horses were prized as military assets. In 1094 the former prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-shengtai">Gao Shengtai</a> forced King Duan Zhengming to give up the throne and renamed the realm the Dazhong Kingdom; when he died in 1096 the Duan family regained it, but the Gao clan stayed in power at court and later fought among themselves.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke Khan</a> sent envoys demanding surrender, and the king of Dali had them killed. Möngke put his brother Kublai in charge of the invasion. Kublai's army crossed the Jinsha River at night and routed the Dali army, and in late 1253 three Mongol columns converged on the city of Dali. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duan-xingzhi">Duan Xingzhi</a> fled to Shanchan and it took two more years of fighting to capture both cities. In 1256 he surrendered, and the Duan family continued to govern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> under the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a>. The Ming conquered Yunnan in 1382.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The administration of Dali has been characterised as a mandala state similar to Southeast Asian kingdoms, with some Chinese influence. The Duan rulers gave Sinitic-style appointments and hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> to clans such as the Gao, Yang and Dong, and allied with the leaders of peripheral peoples. The kings styled themselves Buddha-kings, and ten of the 22 kings retired to become monks. The ruling elite wrote in Chinese script, supplemented by Bai characters, so most sources are in Classical Chinese. Most Bai people trace their ancestry to Nanzhao and Dali, though the records of those kingdoms do not mention them.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dali Kingdom” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
